<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Democratic Party of Socialists of Montenegro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as DPS</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Democratic Party of Socialists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> is a political party founded in 1991 as the successor of the League of Communists of Montenegro. Led for most of its history by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milo-dukanovic">Milo Đukanović</a>, it governed Montenegro without interruption for three decades, first within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Montenegro, then after leading the country to independence in 2006, and taking it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 2017. It lost power in the 2020 election.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party's history begins in the political turmoil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in the late 1980s. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a> took power in the League of Communists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, rallies he organised ousted the leaderships of the League's branches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, an episode known as the Anti-bureaucratic revolution. In Montenegro it brought to power a leadership allied with Milošević, including Momir Bulatović, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milo-dukanovic">Milo Đukanović</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svetozar-marovic">Svetozar Marović</a>.</p><p>The League of Communists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> won the December 1990 election, the first relatively free multi-party election in Socialist Montenegro, taking 83 of 125 seats. It had the whole established party structure at its disposal while its rivals started from nothing. On 22 June 1991 it renamed itself the Democratic Party of Socialists of Montenegro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Bulatović as president the party aligned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, its sole partner in the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> from 1992, and won the elections of 1992 and 1993 and of 1996. In July 1997 the party's national committee chose Milica Pejanović-Đurišić to replace Bulatović as party president, and the split made a confrontation between Đukanović and Bulatović inevitable.</p><p>Đukanović narrowly won the presidential election of October 1997. Bulatović formed the Socialist People's Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> from his defeated faction, which kept a unionist position. Đukanović opposed Milošević, Montenegro received economic aid from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the government gradually took control of customs and the economy and introduced the Deutsche mark and then the euro.</p><p>After Milošević's overthrow on 5 October 2000 the party moved toward independence. A negotiation mediated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2003 set a three-year wait before a referendum, and the party led the pro-independence campaign. With 55.5 per cent in favour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> became independent on 3 June 2006. The party remained the strongest in the elections of 2006, 2009 and 2012, usually governing with the Social Democratic Party of Montenegro and minority parties. Filip Vujanović, a former vice-president of the party, was president of Montenegro from 2002 to 2018, and Đukanović succeeded him in 2018.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At the 2020 parliamentary election the party ran independently and the opposition parties won, ending its thirty years in government. A controversial religious freedom law and corruption scandals had marked the run-up. In 2023 Milatović defeated Đukanović in the presidential election, and Đukanović resigned the party presidency after 24 years. Danijel Živković was elected to succeed him, and the party stood in opposition after the parliamentary election of 11 June 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Democratic Party of Socialists of Montenegro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A constitution adopted in 2007 made Montenegrin the official language. Đukanović's <b>Democratic Party of Socialists</b> dominated politics for three decades, and tourism along the Adriatic coast is the mainstay of the economy.</blockquote><a href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a></li></ul></section>
